it may seem improbable but many coastal  tidal marshes are drowning shamone  Anna's felled director of a  four-year-long research project at the  Yale School of Forestry and  environmental studies has been  monitoring long island sounds tidal  marshes the tidal marshes are critical  to maintaining wildlife habitat and  coastal communities drowning marshes are  areas where we're losing the vegetation  we're losing side of the base of the  food chain the high productivity the  organic material that provides food for  the invertebrates and then the  vertebrate consumers it's ultimately a  degradation a loss of those habitats is  a loss for for the wildlife that depends  on them in addition marshes are thought  to provide an important buffer for  coastal communities against storms  against erosion and as those marshes  themselves are eroded and become open  water systems become tidal mud flat  systems they provide less than the  buffer they don't slow water down as  much sea level rise is or overtaking the  ability of the marsh to keep up so the  sea level is rising more rapidly than  the marsh surfaces rising professor anne  is felled and his team created plots in  guilford and Westport Connecticut to  test whether fertilizers are causing the  drowning what we're doing here is we're  we're actually manipulating the marsh  we're adding nutrients that is we're  adding nitrogen and we're adding  phosphorus sort of fertilizer to the  marsh to see what happens and that is  really to test the hypothesis to test  the idea that too much nutrients is a is  bad for the marsh and can lead to  drowning the possible links between  adding nutrients and drowning are really  twofold we know that adding nitrogen to  marshes will increase the above-ground  production increase the the plant  material that you can see and that's not  going to lead to drowning but our  concern was that it would lead to less  productivity below ground that is less  organic matter less material growing  below the surface and that it would lead  to higher decomposition rates that is  greater loss of material below ground  those two combined or even separately  could lead to less material  in the marsh in the marsh Pete in the  marsh sediment in the marsh soil and  lead to degradation of the marsh and the  logic is simply that the plants use the  roots to get nutrients if they have  higher nutrient available to them they  don't need roots as much so what we set  up here at this stable Marsh were a set  of 24 plots and they were divided into  four different kinds of treatments so  four different things we did one was a  control where we didn't had anything  just monitored the marsh second type of  treatment was adding nitrogen and  monitoring the marsh third was adding  phosphorus and monitoring and the fourth  is adding both nitrogen and phosphorus  together professor Anna's Feld suspects  the lack of sediment rather than the  presence of nutrients could be  contributing to Marsh drowning so what  we've seen at least so far is that the  nutrients certainly make a difference in  terms of the above ground productivity  as we expected and as other people have  shown adding nitrogen does lead to  higher above ground production and you  can see it actually very clearly in the  marsh the nitrogen fertilizer plots or  taller more productive than the controls  and then the phosphorus for less plots  in terms of the below ground which is  really what we were after we didn't see  large differences so at this point we're  not inclined to think that nutrients are  an important part of the of the drowning  equation and we're looking for other  explanations I think we want to look at  next are the ideas that came out of the  first phase of this project which really  did not support the nutrient link but  rather pointed us more in the direction  of inorganic processes that is the  sediment that is being delivered by the  tides the hydrology of the marsh we're  going to continue the fertilization  experiment we want to kind of pin down  the effects of the nutrients here but we  don't think that's the main cause of the  drowning Marsh drowning may be the  result of sea level rise which will  accelerate as the world gets warmer sea  level has been rising in Long Island  Sound for for thousands of years since  the last glaciers were here and as the  glaciers melted a lot more water went  into the sound and that water warmed  over time and so sea levels been rising  in Long Island Sound the water was  rising at about a millimeter a year and  then about 150 years ago at about double  to about two millimeters a year it seems  pretty clear that  that global warming that anthropogenic  climate change is leading to a warming  of the oceans which is going to lead to  an increase in the rate at which sea  level rises for more information about  professor Ana's Feld's work and the Yale  School of Forestry and environmental  studies visit environment yale edu
 

